cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this stack, the benchmark numbers or the protocol, please cite it as below."
title: "local-groundedness-check: LettuceDetect v2 and Granite Guardian as a measured, on-premises groundedness check for RAG answers and document deliverables"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Norton
    given-names: Bradley
    affiliation: "Norton Design Lab"
    email: bradley.norton@nortondesignlab.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/NortonDesignLab/local-groundedness-check"
url: "https://github.com/NortonDesignLab/local-groundedness-check"
license: MIT
version: "1.3.1"
date-released: "2026-10-08"
keywords:
  - hallucination detection
  - groundedness
  - RAG
  - LettuceDetect
  - Granite Guardian
  - HaluBench
  - on-premises
abstract: >-
  Deploy files, an Open WebUI filter, a batch claim checker and a benchmark harness for checking
  that answers built from retrieved documents are supported by those documents, with no text
  leaving the operator's machines. LettuceDetect v2 (2B) screens sentences, IBM Granite Guardian
  4.1-8B judges flagged sentences with the question attached, a general local model adds a second
  vote, and a deterministic orphan-number rule runs on prose sources. On 1,094 label-balanced
  HaluBench items the full configuration reaches 0.796 balanced accuracy and misses 75
  fabrications against 159 for the screen-and-judge pair alone; every component was McNemar-tested.
references:
